WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:06.150
While some moviegoers might argue that the
perfect scene doesn't exist, quite a few Marvel

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.800
movies come darn close to proving that argument
wrong.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:14.380
Buckle up, because we're diving headfirst
into the most memorable, iconic, and practically

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.240
flawless scenes in the MCU.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.320
Iron Man is a masterpiece of a movie.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.650
Robert Downey Jr.'s performance as Tony Stark
is exactly what the MCU needed to gain some

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.160
momentum right out of the gate.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.340
"Yeah, peace.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:27.880
I love peace.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.100
I'd be out of a job with peace."

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.499
While it's a great experience from one end
to the other, though, there's one scene that

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.960
stands out above the rest.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.379
We're talking about Stark's emergence from
captivity, decked out in the towering first

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.380
version of his Iron Man suit, the Mark I.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.300
The clunky armor is a rough start compared
to the nanotech that Stark eventually creates.

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:52.999
It gets stuck in the wall and requires a handmade
spark to launch its missiles.

00:00:52.999 --> 00:00:54.739
And it's not exactly durable.

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:58.679
But it's all he needs to blow that popsicle
stand and then hightail it out of there, leaving

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.540
the astonished survivors of the Ten Rings
in the rearview mirror.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:07.040
The death of Yinsen adds some emotional fuel
to the fire, too, as he implores Stark not

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:08.040
to waste his life.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.420
"Thank you for saving me."

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.000
"Don't waste it — don't waste your life."

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.560
The emergence of the MCU's first bona fide
superhero was pulled off with a precision

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.280
that simultaneously cut to the quick and had
us on our feet cheering.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:30.220
Sometimes it's an unspoken understanding between
characters that makes a scene truly flawless.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.660
Few action movie moments have done this as
well as Steve Rogers' reckless sacrifice at

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.940
training camp, when he throws himself on a
grenade that he doesn't know is a dummy.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:45.320
This moment in Captain America: The First
Avenger punctuates a debate between Colonel

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:50.460
Chester Phillips and Dr. Abraham Erskine about
who should be recruited as the first "super

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.420
soldier" in their program.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.759
Phillips casually tosses the grenade into
the midst of the new recruits, at which point

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:57.900
everyone scrambles.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:01:59.480
The one exception?

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:00.040
Rogers.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:01.840
"Get away!

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:07.240
The scene is a splendid illustration of Rogers'
ideals, all wrapped up into a single action.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.760
Compare that to the moment in Endgame when
he faces down Thanos' entire army or the numerous

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.620
occasions in which he professed that he could,
quote, "do this all day."

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.480
That guy started as a skinny fella with a
heart of gold, ready to jump on a grenade

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.800
if that's what was required.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:29.720
Even a superheroic transformation, decades
spent frozen in ice, and cosmic confrontations

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.360
with unimaginable evil couldn't take that
away from him.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:37.840
Everything hits the fan when Thanos arrives
on the scene in Avengers: Infinity War.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.420
While Endgame would eventually even the score
by dusting the Mad Titan and seeing the good

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.629
guys back on top, its predecessor marks one
of the first times Marvel actually ended things

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.620
on a sour note — and we mean a really sour
note.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.000
The story comes to a fever pitch as Thanos
plucks the Mind Stone out of Vision's forehead,

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.680
completing his gauntlet, and then snaps his
fingers after Thor fails to finish him off.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.860
The sequence that follows takes us across
the universe, showing the effects of the snap

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:16.940
as half of all life in existence crumbles
to ashes on the spot.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:22.340
One moment on Titan is particularly emotional
as Spider-Man bids a desperate farewell to

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:23.780
his mentor, Tony Stark.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:25.440
"I'm sorry."

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:39.000
Back on Earth, Steve Rogers' slow realization
that they have just lost is just as moving,

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.760
as he sits, slumped on the ground in defeat
with his friends turned to dust around him.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:50.180
While it may not be fun, the Decimation is
one of the most flawlessly executed moments

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:51.060
in the entire MCU.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:57.160
Directors Joe and Anthony Russo have brought
us many of the MCU's most compelling moments,

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.220
and their work makes it onto this list more
than once.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.860
A particular highlight, though, is the Captain
America: Civil War's climactic confrontation.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.660
The final moments of the film see Steve Rogers
and Bucky Barnes face off against a furious

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.879
Tony Stark, who has just discovered that it
was none other than a brainwashed Barnes himself

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:16.780
who killed his parents.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:22.660
The scene is set against a cold, hard backdrop
of snow and stone as the heroes tangle through

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.740
an abandoned Cold War facility in Siberia.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:30.800
Stark's superior armor seems to put him at
an advantage, but it's ultimately Rogers'

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.160
attitude that helps him defeat the Iron Avenger
by putting a shield through his arc reactor.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.610
From stellar cinematography to the wrenching
emotional conflict to the choreography of

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:43.540
the fight itself, everything about this scene
is a home run.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:52.900
Michael Rooker's Yondu Udonta steals the show
for a lot of Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:53.440
2.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:58.020
However, the scene in which he stages a jailbreak
and then retakes his entire ship with the

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:01.820
help of Rocket and Baby Groot is particularly
priceless.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:06.330
The scene, cheekily set to the needle drop
of Jay and the Americans' "Come a Little Bit

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:09.620
Closer," perfectly embodies the spirit of
the Guardians franchise.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.580
Irreverent, careless, mindlessly violent,
unpredictable, self-destructive, and downright

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:20.180
hilarious, the sequence shows Yondu's Arrow
speed around the ship, hunting his mutinous

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:25.860
crew one by one until he finally takes out
Taserface… and the entire ship to boot.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:31.460
The legendary feat firmly establishes Yondu
as a Mary Poppins-tier hero, y'all… and

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:35.060
just in time for him to make the sacrifice
play as the film wraps up.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:39.740
The scene is deliciously tongue-in-cheek and
masterfully leaves the audience struggling

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.160
with a brand of morbid humor that only James
Gunn can so casually invoke.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:51.240
"You maniac…"

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:57.000
The airport fight scene in Captain America:
Civil War is incredible on so many levels.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:02.720
Though they've fought one-on-one plenty of
times, this is the one and only occasion that

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.440
the MCU's heroes have taken each other on
as teams.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:08.820
And it's positively dripping with quality
action beats.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.080
Naturally, the fighting is preceded by some
light banter, because even at their lowest

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:17.200
points, Marvel's heroes can't contain their
sarcasm.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:22.660
Once the joking is finished, though, the divided
group gets down to business, and boy is it

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:23.200
fun to watch.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:25.060
"Underoos!"

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:31.120
"Nice job, kid!"

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:32.300
"Thanks!"

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.540
The scene has Tom Holland's Spider-Man swinging
into action for the first time, even nabbing

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:38.820
Cap's shield while he's at it.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:44.860
Scarlet Witch continues to showcase her impressive
powerset as well, and newcomer T'Challa cuts

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:47.120
a dashing figure in his vibranium Black Panther
suit.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.920
Even Ant-Man makes a huge splash by transforming
into Giant-Man and wrecking everything in

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:53.500
his path.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.520
The thrilling moments just keep on coming
with this one.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.820
From start to finish, the airport fight is
one of the most exciting — and funniest

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:04.780
— action sequences that the MCU has ever
produced.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:06.360
"I'm really sorry."

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:11.120
“You couldn't have done that earlier?"

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:13.240
"......I hate you."

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.820
Thor's character has come a long way since
those early Phase 1 days.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.480
While his second solo outing, Thor: The Dark
World, is not exactly a fan favorite, the

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.880
God of Thunder's story had new life breathed
into it with Thor: Ragnarok.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:33.819
In this wild adventure, the hero finds himself
stranded and enslaved on the garbage planet

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:34.420
of Sakaar.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:40.240
This ultimately leads to his being forced
to don armor and enter a gladiatorial arena

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.889
to face the Grandmaster's Champion, who turns
out to be none other than Hulk.

00:07:44.889 --> 00:07:48.520
The entire scene in the arena is perfectly
executed, from Thor's …

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:49.600
“Hey, hey!

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:52.480
We know each other.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:55.169
He's a friend from work!"

00:07:55.169 --> 00:07:58.620
... line to the movie's nod to everybody's
favorite scene from Avengers …

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:02.100
"Yes!

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:03.620
That's how it feels!"

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.940
… to Thor and Hulk's raucous brawl that
ends with the so-called "Lord of Thunder"

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.940
electrifying the Jade Giant before being incapacitated
by his obedience disc.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:18.220
The scene also serves as a sweet shout out
to Hulk's own Planet Hulk story from the comics,

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:22.460
all while kicking off his own three-part story
arc of self-discovery within the MCU.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:28.380
The first Avengers movie follows Earth's Mightiest
Heroes as they get together for the first

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:33.520
time, iron out their differences, and stop
a Chitauri invasion of New York City led by

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:35.000
Thor's trickster brother Loki.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:40.400
With so many great scenes unfolding throughout
the film, though, there's one that has to

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:45.510
be called out every time: the shot of the
six heroes, suited up together in the ruined

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:46.660
streets of the Big Apple.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:52.020
With a portal open above the city and the
fate of the world at stake, the team finally

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:56.300
gets their act together just in time for one
of Steve Rogers' first great moments as a

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:57.720
leader of the Avengers.

00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:03.540
With Tony Stark's blessing, Cap takes charge,
assigning roles to each hero and helping contain

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:07.880
the unfolding disaster long enough for the
Iron Avenger to fly an inbound missile through

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.800
the wormhole to wrap things up.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:11.000
"And Hulk."

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:14.520
"Smash."

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:20.200
But it all starts with that one shot, circling
around the six heroes as they stand, back

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.020
to back, ready to fight and die doing their
duty.

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:28.180
Marvel had been building to that exact moment
for four straight years, and it serves as

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:29.760
a perfect crescendo.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:35.300
The MCU has a lot of last-minute arrivals
and nick-of-time heroic entrances that make

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.540
you want to get up out of your chair and start
cheering at the top of your lungs.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:42.960
But there's one scene, in particular, that
has to make it onto the shortlist of greats:

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:46.600
Thor's arrival in Wakanda in Avengers: Infinity
War.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:51.760
After spending the bulk of the movie off on
his own side quest in search of a new weapon,

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:56.340
the God of Thunder's arrival on the battle-swept
plains of Wakanda is literally electrifying.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:01.980
It breathes new life into his allies and utterly
terrifies his enemies — those that don't

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:04.240
get blown to smithereens within the first
few seconds, at least.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:06.640
His added war cry of …

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:09.220
"Bring me Thanos!"

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:13.980
… sends shivers down the spine as he charges
into the fray with Stormbreaker in hand.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:19.040
The entire event is an optimistic beat in
a movie that generally grows ever bleaker

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:23.330
from the beginning onward, and the thrilling
change of pace is inserted into the narrative

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:24.120
at the perfect moment.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:29.720
Sometimes the best scenes come in the form
of unexpected sidebars between characters.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.410
In Thor: Ragnarok, the tensions are high when
Thor is thrown into the Grandmaster's prison

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:36.940
after arriving on Sakaar.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:41.400
He knows that he must face a champion and
still, somehow, find a way to stop his sister

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:41.960
Hela.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:47.540
And yet, with so much at stake, the narrative
pauses to introduce two new lovable characters:

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:52.500
Korg and Miek, fellow gladiators who quickly
bond with the Asgardian.

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.580
"I'm made of rocks as you can see, but don't
let that intimidate you.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.920
You don't have to be afraid unless you're
made of scissors.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:10:59.460
Hoo-hoo.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:01.760
Little rock, paper, scissors joke for ya."

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:06.740
Upon their first meeting after Thor arrives
in the "freaky circle," Korg informs the God

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:11.621
of Thunder about his newfound captivity, fills
him in on numerous details about himself and

00:11:11.621 --> 00:11:15.670
his friend Miek, and even goes off on a tangent
about a failed revolution that he tried to

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:16.220
start up.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:20.440
"Ah, well, I tried to start a revolution but
didn't print enough pamphlets, so hardly anyone

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.480
turned up except for my mum and her boyfriend
— who I hate."

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:29.300
The impressive thing about the scene is how
seamlessly it's woven into the greater story,

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:32.960
casually introducing two lighthearted characters
who end up having a massive impact on Thor's

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:33.500
destiny.

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:40.300
Winter Soldier gradually turns up the tension
as Captain America begins to realize the full

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:43.480
extent to which S.H.I.E.L.D. has been infiltrated
by Hydra.

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:46.240
It all comes to a head during a long elevator
ride.

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:52.160
The slow tension of the scene ramps up beautifully
as Cap stands, hands crossed, while more and

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:57.080
more bruisers step onto the lift, filling
it up to maximum capacity.

00:11:57.660 --> 00:11:58.200
His line …

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:02.660
"Before we start, does anyone want to get
out?"

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:06.860
… is the perfect kick-off to a fight of
claustrophobically epic proportions.

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:11.380
Within moments, Rogers takes on the entire
elevator, carpeting the floor with his opponents'

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:12.280
knocked out bodies.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:18.070
The final line — quote, "It kind of feels
personal" — followed by the kick-and-catch

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:22.510
of his shield is pure gravy for a scene that
leaves no doubt that Steve Rogers is a guy

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:23.900
you don't want to mess with.

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:29.170
He may be soft and warm on the outside, but
backed into a corner — or an elevator — the

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:30.820
guy simply can't be brought down.

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.220
"I can do this all day..."

